# work-wiki environment variables.
# Copy to `.env.local` (Next dev) and/or `.dev.vars` (wrangler/OpenNext local).
# Both files are gitignored. Production sets these as Cloudflare Worker
# secrets/vars and (for NEXT_PUBLIC_* values) in the deploy build step.

# --- Clerk auth (email + optional X/Twitter SSO) ---
# Both are required at runtime, even reads, or the app 500s.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_test_...
CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_...
# After sign-in always land on the Workbench (`/`). Fallback alone loses to a
# Clerk `redirect_url` (dashboard After sign-in URL, or middleware return-back
# to an old chrome route). Force wins. Match these in `.env.local`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_URL=/sign-in
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_FALLBACK_REDIRECT_URL=/
NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_SIGN_IN_FORCE_REDIRECT_URL=/
#
# Invite-only / waitlist is configured in the CLERK DASHBOARD, not via env:
#   1. Enable Email auth (needed for waitlist invitation emails).
#   2. Require Username at sign-up — the preferred /u/<handle> URL basis;
#      resolveHandle() in src/lib/auth.ts falls back to the X handle / user id
#      if it's unset, so the app still runs, just with less stable handles.
#   3. Waitlist page → toggle "Enable waitlist" (gates new registration only;
#      reading the commons stays public). Approve people via the row "…" → Invite.
# The app side is just `waitlistUrl="/waitlist"` on <ClerkProvider> (layout.tsx)
# + the /waitlist page — no extra env var.

# --- Site owner ---
# TWO vars name the owner, and a local deployment wants BOTH — otherwise its
# route gates behave differently from production (see wrangler.jsonc, which sets
# both). Since DW-486 the STABLE Clerk id is what decides owner-ness wherever it
# is configured; the handle is the fallback.
#
# The owner's stable Clerk user id (`user_…`). SERVER-ONLY — never inlined into
# the client bundle. It gates the whole private deployment in middleware AND, via
# `isOwnerPrincipal`, every server-side owner gate (settings, lint, wiki
# creation, Schema edits, admin routes, the owner⇒admin page grant). It cannot
# drift: a Clerk username change, or a user with no username and no linked X
# account, leaves it alone. Unset → middleware fails closed (503) and the route
# gates fall back to the handle below.
YOPEDIA_OWNER_USER_ID=user_...

# The X/Twitter handle of the site owner. Public (inlined into the client bundle
# at build time), which is why the three client islands (nav, article actions,
# revision history) gate on it — they cannot see the server-only id above.
# It is also the FALLBACK the server gates use when no owner id is configured,
# and the only fact a bearer service principal carries.
# With NEITHER var set, nobody is the owner — including the deployer.
NEXT_PUBLIC_OWNER_HANDLE=yuanhao

# --- LLM + vision (generation, image description) ---
# DeepSeek (multimodal, used for ingest synthesis and image vision).
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=sk-...
# Optional overrides:
# LLM_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash
# VISION_MODEL=@cf/llava-hf/llava-1.5-7b-hf   # Workers AI fallback when no LLM key
#
# The `custom` provider: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint you point at yourself.
# Both halves are required — a key with no endpoint has nowhere to go, and an
# endpoint with no key is a request that 401s — and there is no default model
# name, so set one in Settings → LLM Models. Either half may instead be stored
# through that surface; these vars win when set.
# LLM_CUSTOM_BASE_URL=https://api.example.com/v1
# LLM_CUSTOM_API_KEY=sk-...

# --- Embeddings + vector search (optional) ---
# Vector search is OFF until it is switched on in Settings → Embeddings, and the
# switch itself refuses until an embedding provider, a model that sits in that
# provider's namespace (see EMBEDDING_MODEL) and — for OpenAI and Google — an
# endpoint AND a key are all present. There is no env var for the endpoint or for
# the switch, so a deployment configured only from here supplies the
# provider/model/key legs and turns it on from the surface.
# These two vars win when set and a save cannot move them; the endpoint and the
# stored key are edited in Settings → Embeddings.
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai        # openai | google | ollama | workers-ai
#   `workers-ai` reaches Cloudflare through the `AI` binding, so it works ONLY on
#   the Workers runtime; anywhere else it is dropped silently and embeddings are
#   off. `openai`/`google` need their key below or embeddings are equally off —
#   an override that cannot resolve does not fall back to auto-detection.
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
#   Model ids are namespaced: `@cf/…` for `workers-ai`, non-`@cf/` for every
#   other provider. A mismatch is refused by the Settings switch and, on the
#   embedding path, ignored in favour of the provider's default model.
# The key is the provider's own: OPENAI_API_KEY or GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY,
# counted per provider — an OpenAI key does not satisfy a Google selection. A
# set-but-EMPTY value counts as unset, so the stored key applies instead.
# Ollama and Workers AI are keyless and carry their own transport, so neither an
# endpoint nor a key is asked of them.

# --- Deep Research search provider (optional) ---
# One provider searches at a time, and it is the one that is SELECTED. A
# selection whose credential is missing FAILS the run and names itself — no
# other provider is used in its place, even when its key is sitting right here.
# All of these are also settable in Settings → External Sources; the vars win.
# RESEARCH_PROVIDER=tavily         # tavily | serpapi | searxng (default tavily)
# TAVILY_API_KEY=tvly-...
#   The default provider. Returns each result's own page text, so no second
#   fetch is needed per source.
# SERPAPI_API_KEY=...
# SERPAPI_ENGINE=google
#   SerpApi returns snippets only; each result URL is then extracted through the
#   same readability path a browser clip uses.
# SEARXNG_BASE_URL=https://searx.example.com
# SEARXNG_CATEGORIES=general
#   Categories are your instance's own vocabulary; leave unset for its default.
# SEARXNG_API_KEY=...
#   Optional bearer token for a private instance. Env only — no Settings field.

# --- Firecrawl (optional; Capture only) ---
# The optional Capture credential for hosted page fetches. It is NOT a Deep
# Research search provider — those are the three above. Also settable in
# Settings → External Sources; this var wins when set.
# FIRECRAWL_API_KEY=fc-...

# --- Hermes Agent orchestration (optional) ---
# Root URL of a separately hosted Hermes API server (do not include /v1).
# HERMES_AGENT_URL=https://hermes.example.com
# API_SERVER_KEY from the Hermes host; set as a Worker secret in production.
# HERMES_API_KEY=...
# HERMES_MODEL=hermes-agent

# --- Trusted-memory operations (optional) ---
# HMAC-sign outbound integration webhooks. Use a long random Worker secret.
# YOPEDIA_WEBHOOK_SIGNING_SECRET=...
# Optional USD rates per million tokens used only for approximate Agent Studio
# receipts. Leave unset to show token usage without a potentially misleading
# estimate; update when the selected model's pricing changes.
# LLM_INPUT_COST_PER_MILLION=...
# LLM_OUTPUT_COST_PER_MILLION=...

# --- YouTube transcript (optional) ---
# Only needed when direct transcript fetch is blocked by YouTube.
# YOUTUBE_TRANSCRIPT_API_KEY=...

# --- X / Twitter ingestion (optional) ---
# X API v2 bearer token. Plain tweets read fine without it (public syndication
# CDN), but long-form X *Articles* only expose a ~200-char teaser there — their
# full body needs this token. Set it on the MAIN app Worker
# (`wrangler secret put X_BEARER_TOKEN`) so a pasted / MCP-ingested X Article
# fetches its full body; without it, articles silently degrade to the teaser.
# Needs a tier with recent-search/article access, and the body is only reachable
# within ~7 days of posting (X's recent-search window).
# X_BEARER_TOKEN=...

# --- Analytics (PostHog page views) ---
# Page-view tracking is ON by default with a built-in PUBLIC project key.
# Override the key/host here (NEXT_PUBLIC_* are inlined at BUILD time, so set
# them in the deploy build env, not just at runtime). Pageviews only — cookieless
# (memory persistence, no consent banner), no autocapture, no session replay.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# --- Local sidecar (optional) ---
# Local Chat can use the owner's signed-in Codex / Claude Code subscriptions.
# Set this in the shared .env.local for BOTH Next.js and the sidecar. In
# Settings → LLM Models choose OpenAI or Anthropic as Chat provider; an empty
# Chat model uses the selected CLI's default. Install and sign in to the official
# clients first (`codex login`, `claude auth login`). Plan usage limits apply.
# This applies to local Workbench Chat only, not ingest, embeddings or cloud LLM
# calls. The default remains direct API access. No API-key fallback is attempted
# in subscription mode. Never put subscription OAuth tokens in this file.
# WORKWIKI_CHAT_TRANSPORT=subscription
#
# READ BY THE SIDECAR PROCESS, NOT BY THE WORKER. Everything above this block
# follows the header at the top of this file — copy to `.env.local` / `.dev.vars`,
# set in production as a Cloudflare Worker secret or var. This one does not.
# `pnpm sidecar` (node sidecar/server.mjs) runs on the owner's own machine and
# reads the REPO ROOT's `.env` and then `.env.local` at startup, taking the
# first definition it finds; a value already exported in the shell that launched
# it wins over both. `.dev.vars` is never read, and setting this as a Worker
# secret or var does nothing at all — the Worker never sees the sidecar's door.
# The sidecar binds 127.0.0.1:19828 over plain HTTP and is probed by the BROWSER
# (the Worker cannot reach localhost). Its CORS door decides which page may talk
# to it.
# With nothing set here it admits only this machine: localhost, 127.0.0.1 and
# the IPv6 loopback literal [::1], on either scheme and any port. A local dev
# server therefore needs NO entry.
# Set this only when the Workbench is served from a DEPLOYED origin that must
# reach a sidecar on the owner's machine. Comma-separated bare origins
# (scheme://host[:port]) — no path, no query, no credentials, no "*" wildcard.
# Matching is exact on the normalized origin, so https://app.example does not
# admit https://app.example.evil.test. An entry that is not a bare origin is
# DROPPED (and logged) rather than fatal: a typo must never stop the sidecar
# binding, and the fallback is the loopback-only behaviour above.
# Naming an origin opens EVERY /api/v1 route to it, not just health — Chat,
# which drives the local Agent and shell, plus the workspace file routes, Skills
# and the kernel proxy — with the loopback token as the only remaining barrier.
# A plaintext http:// entry can be forged by anyone on the path, who then
# inherits that whole surface.
# WORKWIKI_SIDECAR_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://app.example,https://notes.example
